---
// Fundations
import Text from "@/components/fundations/elements/Text.astro";
import Button from "@/components/fundations/elements/Button.astro";
import Wrapper from "@/components/fundations/containers/Wrapper.astro";
---

<section>
  <Wrapper variant="standard" class="py-24">
    <!-- Top: mascot + intro side by side -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-12 lg:gap-16">
      <!-- Mascot image -->
      <div class="flex items-center justify-center lg:justify-start">
        <img
          src="https://cdn.hob.farm/pages/home/hob-mascot.jpg"
          alt="The Hob, HobFarm's resident maker"
          width="360"
          height="360"
          class="rounded-lg border border-base-800"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>

      <!-- Main content -->
      <div class="lg:col-span-2">
        <Text tag="span" variant="textSM" class="text-accent-500 font-mono">
          MEET THE HOB
        </Text>
        <Text
          tag="h2"
          variant="displayLG"
          class="mt-4 text-white font-display"
        >
          The Hob
        </Text>
        <div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
          <Text tag="p" variant="textBase" class="text-base-400 text-pretty">
            In the folklore of the North York Moors, a hob is a household spirit.
            Not mischievous like elves, not menacing like boggarts. Helpful. A hob
            shows up uninvited, does the work nobody asked for, and disappears
            before dawn. Churns the butter, mends the fence, finishes the half-done
            chores. You never see it work. You just see the results.
          </Text>
          <Text tag="p" variant="textBase" class="text-base-400 text-pretty">
            The tradition came with rules. Leave out an offering of cream (full-fat,
            never skimmed, or you'd feel their wrath). Never spy on them. And never,
            under any circumstances, give them clothing; a clothed hob takes offense
            and leaves, never to return.
          </Text>
          <Text tag="p" variant="textBase" class="text-base-400 text-pretty">
            The North York Moors is still marked with their names. Hob Hole, Hob's
            Beck, Boggle Hole. In the 1820s, a man named George Calvert walked the
            region collecting their stories before they faded: T'Hob o' Hobgarth,
            the Cross Hob of Lastingham, Hodge Hob o' Bransdale, dozens more. Each
            one tied to a specific place, a specific household, a specific kind of
            help.
          </Text>
          <Text tag="p" variant="textBase" class="text-base-400 text-pretty">
            That's the idea behind HobFarm. The "farm" part pulls triple duty:
            a farm full of hobs tending to creative tasks, a server farm running
            autonomous workflows, and the folklore tradition of leaving offerings
            so the spirits keep working. Feed the system good inputs, and the
            hobs do the rest.
          </Text>
          <Text tag="p" variant="textSM" class="text-base-500 mt-2">
            The hob tradition originates in the folklore of the North York Moors.
            To learn more about the helpful hobs and the Calvert Manuscript, visit the
            <Text
              tag="a"
              variant="textSM"
              href="https://www.ryedalefolkmuseum.co.uk/the-helpful-hobs-of-the-north-york-moors/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class="text-base-400 hover:text-white underline decoration-base-600 hover:decoration-white transition-colors"
            >
              Ryedale Folk Museum
            </Text>.
          </Text>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Bottom: expanded context in two columns -->
    <div class="mt-16 grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-12">
      <!-- The Character -->
      <div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-4">
          <div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-accent-500"></div>
          <Text tag="h3" variant="textSM" class="text-white font-mono">
            The Character
          </Text>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <Text tag="p" variant="textSM" class="text-base-400 text-pretty">
            He's been a mechanic with grease on his hands and a server rack behind
            him. A noir detective under sodium lights. A medieval tinkerer soldering
            something that shouldn't exist yet. The setting changes; the face
            doesn't. That's the point.
          </Text>
          <Text tag="p" variant="textSM" class="text-base-400 text-pretty">
            The Hob is built entirely through prompt engineering and reference
            management, not fine-tuning or custom models. Same bone structure, same
            attitude, same presence, regardless of what world he lands in. Drop him
            into a cyberpunk alley or a Renaissance workshop and you'd still
            recognize him. That consistency is what StyleFusion is designed to
            produce, and the Hob is the longest-running proof that the system works.
          </Text>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- The Companions -->
      <div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-4">
          <div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-accent-500"></div>
          <Text tag="h3" variant="textSM" class="text-white font-mono">
            The Companions
          </Text>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <Text tag="p" variant="textSM" class="text-base-400 text-pretty">
            Stories have always been built around parties, not solo acts. The hero
            needs the trickster, the sage, the wildcard. It's not just narrative
            tradition; it's how audiences orient themselves in a world. Each
            companion is a lens on a different part of the story.
          </Text>
          <Text tag="p" variant="textSM" class="text-base-400 text-pretty">
            HobFarm works the same way. The Hob lives on the website and builds
            things. HobGal handles social media and public-facing work. Buffcock
            exists to cause problems. More will follow as the world grows, each one
            filling a role in how the ecosystem communicates.
          </Text>
          <Text tag="p" variant="textSM" class="text-base-400 text-pretty">
            These aren't just mascots. They're a demonstration of the core problem
            HobFarm solves: maintaining identity and consistency across tools that
            have no memory. Each character is a walking portfolio piece, showing
            that you can build a coherent visual world with off-the-shelf AI tools
            if you bring the right system to the table.
          </Text>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12">
      <Button isLink size="sm" variant="muted" href="/about">
        About HobFarm &rarr;
      </Button>
    </div>
  </Wrapper>
</section>
